as the january 6th hearings highlight  our nation's political divisions several  former military leaders including  retired army general stanley mcchrystal  and army general doug lute are urging  americans to recommit to this country's  core principles they are part of a group  called team democracy that wants people  to pledge that elections are conducted  according to the rule of law without  partisan bias the general spoke with  senior investigative correspondent  catherine herrich about the new  initiative and why they believe our  democracy faces its greatest threat  since the civil war  i actually think that this is as  dangerous a time as we've seen in our  lifetime retired army general stanley  mcchrystal who once led u.s forces  around the globe including afghanistan  sees america's current divisions as an  issue of national security because you  see the symptoms of people who are  disassociated from the legitimacy of the  government they are starting to question  basic things like was an election  outcome valid i'm beginning to see as  stan suggested these fissures retired  lieutenant general doug lute also served  globally over a four decade career  including as u.s ambassador to nato  these tiny fractures these small  fractures that because of our  international experience we understand  can widen and can be and can divide  america it can actually present  opportunities for our opponents it's not  every day that you have two retired  generals sit down and talk about the  state of our elections why are you  coming forward  our enemies would like nothing more than  to find us  not unified to find us fragmented and  unable to be the kind of america that  they have faced before or relied on it  their allies i think stan and i have  seen democracies fledgling democracies  overseas struggle to find their feet and  at least for me i see some of those  warning signs today in america  as seasoned observers of conflict these  generals joined team democracy to  highlight core principles the idea is to  give every american a reminder  and a commitment that safe and fair  elections are fundamental to our nation  and democracy the election was rigged it  was stolen without the backing of former  president trump and his supporters how  can this work well it starts with the  grassroots movement every american has  got to step back and say what do i  really believe in about our nation and  that is that we are a democracy what  signals that the fact that i believe and  participate in the elections the thing  that unites us is that before we were  supporters of president trump or  president biden or obama we were  americans we are americans  fearful of america's military  superiority these generals say our  adversaries use information as a weapon  to mislead confuse and divide if i were  an adversary of the united states i want  to go after two things first would be  our political unity our ability to make  unified decisions and execute things but  i'd also want to go after our legitimacy  as a nation that other nations want to  emulate from an intelligence perspective  why are americans so divided when people  get messages that are very direct and  often they are either intentionally or  unintentionally incorrect it changes  their beliefs and so they start to hold  fundamental ideas that are contrary to  facts it actually opens opportunities  for those who do us harm and eventually  their aim is to make us so divided that  we're internally consumed we're  self-absorbed with our own problems and  we're unable to address issues overseas  recent cbs news polling underscores the  divisions with most americans expecting  violence after future presidential  elections this is not the america that  we've come to expect of ourselves i  think if we look in cases where people  think violence is an option or even an  obligation then we are starting to have  drifted somewhere that we see in other  countries far away but it's suddenly  come home and we need to pay close  attention to it and once that drift  begins it's very difficult to reverse it  so now is the time as a preventive  measure we need to get in front of these  divides  and try to cement ourselves back  together  the generals have not announced a  complete plan of action yet but believe  asking people from across the political  spectrum to recommit to core democratic  principles is a good starting point and  the group cites research that public  pledges can drive behavior especially  when paired with public accountability  efforts michelle the nation is at stake  catherine thank you
 

